    "seo": {
      "title": "Tepe Sialk: Settlement and Craft near Kashan | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Tepe Sialk’s prehistoric mounds, pottery technology, Iron Age cemeteries, and the uncertainties behind its archaeological interpretation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mounds-and-periods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A landscape of successive settlements",
        "content": "Tepe Sialk lies near Kashan, beside an oasis on the edge of the Dasht-e Kavir, Iran’s Great Salt Desert. Its position connected settlement with routes across the central plateau. Archaeologists distinguish a North Mound, a South Mound, and separate burial grounds. These components should not be compressed into one uninterrupted urban history. [s1]\n\nA British Institute of Persian Studies conference report places North Mound occupation approximately between 6000 and 4900 BCE, and South Mound occupation from around 4100 BCE into the first millennium BCE. These are broad archaeological ranges, not precise founding and abandonment dates. Excavations began under Roman Ghirshman in the 1930s; later Iranian investigations revisited both the sequence and its interpretation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "pottery-technology",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Pottery as evidence of technical knowledge",
        "content": "Sialk’s ceramics reveal more than changing decoration. A laboratory study of North Mound sherds examined their chemical composition, mineral phases, and microscopic structure. Its published abstract reports a gradual development from Sialk I to Sialk II pottery, culminating in substantial production of red pottery. [s2]\n\nThe researchers associate this development with increasingly controlled selection of raw materials and management of firing temperature and atmosphere. A pot’s final appearance therefore reflects decisions made throughout production, rather than painting alone. The evidence supports specialized technical knowledge; it does not, by itself, establish the number of workshops, the organization of labor, or the existence of full-time professional potters. Detailed analytical results beyond the accessible abstract have not been used here. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iron-age-burials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the Iron Age through a pouring vessel",
        "content": "A ceramic pitcher in the Metropolitan Museum of Art offers a more focused view of later Sialk. Catalogued as excavated at the site and dated approximately 900–700 BCE, it has a burnished grey body, a high neck, a handle, and a long projecting spout. Its body was wheel-made, with the handle and spout attached separately. [s3]\n\nThe museum connects this vessel with Necropolis B, a cemetery outside the fortified settlement. Graves there contained ceramic vessels alongside ornaments, weapons, and horse equipment. Such associations help reconstruct burial practice, although objects deposited with the dead do not represent every aspect of ordinary household life. The museum proposes that the pitcher poured a drink containing sediment, possibly wine, and may have served in a funerary ceremony. Those suggestions remain interpretations of its form and context, rather than a directly observed account of its use. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpretation-and-preservation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why interpretations remain open",
        "content": "Even Sialk’s conspicuous mud-brick construction has prompted competing explanations. The BIPS report records a challenge to its identification as a third-millennium BCE ziggurat: a conference presenter argued for a first-millennium BCE platform on ceramic and brick evidence. This is a documented scholarly disagreement, not a settled identification. [s1]\n\nSialk is best understood by keeping different kinds of evidence together: settlement layers, laboratory studies, and recorded burial assemblages. Each answers different questions, while leaving room for revised chronology and interpretation. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
