    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "The archaeology of historical Lorestan includes settlements, sanctuaries, and cemeteries across the western Zagros, extending beyond the boundaries of the present province. Excavated contexts are essential for understanding the metalwork commonly called Luristan bronzes, much of which reached collections without reliable find records.",
    "tags": ["Lorestan", "Zagros", "Archaeology", "Iron Age", "Luristan Bronzes"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T11:56:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T11:56:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Archaeological Sites of Lorestan | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Lorestan through excavated cemeteries, the Surkh Dum sanctuary, Baba Jan settlement, and the provenance problems surrounding Luristan bronzes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A region, not a single civilization",
        "content": "Historical Luristan, here spelled Lorestan except in established archaeological terminology, encompasses a wider region than today’s Lorestan Province. Scholarship on its characteristic bronzes includes sites in present-day Ilam. The archaeological landscape contains different settlement histories and kinds of site; a regional label does not establish a single ancient ethnic identity. [s1]\n\nA useful starting distinction is between an object’s appearance and its documented origin. The familiar term “Luristan bronzes” denotes a particular repertoire of metalwork, but has also been applied incorrectly to objects from other regions or periods. An excavated find can be related to a grave, building, or associated pottery. A market attribution often cannot provide those connections. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "settlement-and-sanctuary",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Baba Jan and Surkh Dum answer different questions",
        "content": "Baba Jan, also written Baba Djan, supplies settlement evidence. Its principal Iron Age occupation included a fortified manor and a fort associated with a religious complex. This helps place regional material culture within inhabited and constructed spaces, rather than treating it entirely as grave furniture. [s1]\n\nSurkh Dum supplies a different context. Erich Schmidt’s excavation in 1938 uncovered a sanctuary containing votive offerings: objects deposited in a religious setting. Decorated pins are particularly important there. Their presence makes it unsafe to assume that every ornate bronze had the same function or came from a burial. Settlement, sanctuary, and cemetery finds belong to different archaeological arguments. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bard-e-bal",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bard-e Bal: the value of recorded burials",
        "content": "Bard-e Bal, in the historical Pusht-i Kuh region near Ilam, was excavated by the Belgian archaeological mission in 1969–1970. Louis Vanden Berghe’s account records seventy tombs, generally stone-built cists, some with substantial slab doors. Many contained more than one burial, and earlier graves were reused. A cemetery can therefore preserve successive acts of burial rather than a single moment. [s2]\n\nThe excavation also documented differences between object categories: in its Iron Age II graves, weapons remained bronze while personal ornaments included iron. Such evidence complicates any simple story in which iron immediately replaced bronze everywhere. Characteristic axes, animal-shaped whetstone handles, and finials occurred in recorded contexts, making the cemetery valuable for dating regional metalwork. Vanden Berghe assigned these examples approximately to the twelfth–tenth centuries BCE; this is the published interpretation of that assemblage, not a universal date for all Luristan bronzes. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "provenance-and-meaning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What lost context prevents us from knowing",
        "content": "The modern collecting history creates a major limitation. Oscar White Muscarella’s survey stresses that most known bronzes entered circulation through looting and the antiquities market, leaving only a small proportion with scientifically recorded contexts. A visually impressive collection is consequently not a representative sample of an excavated community. [s3]\n\nWithout reliable find records, it becomes difficult to establish which objects were deposited together, how their use changed, or how a particular design related to a local practice. Recognizing an animal motif does not resolve those questions. Lorestan’s sites matter because they restore some of the relationships that isolated objects have lost. Their strongest contribution is contextual: they connect craftsmanship with places and practices while exposing how much remains uncertain. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "contexts-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Different contexts support different interpretations",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Recorded archaeological contexts\"] -->|\"Settlement evidence\"| B[\"Baba Jan\"]\n  A -->|\"Sanctuary evidence\"| C[\"Surkh Dum\"]\n  A -->|\"Burial evidence\"| D[\"Bard-e Bal\"]\n  B -->|\"Documents\"| E[\"Buildings and occupation\"]\n  C -->|\"Documents\"| F[\"Votive deposition\"]\n  D -->|\"Documents\"| G[\"Burials and associated objects\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows identify the kinds of evidence supplied by three excavated sites. They do not imply common political authority, identical dates, or a single function for regional bronzes.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
